  <!-- ===== C12 — STAGED (NOT LOADED): privileged-group add shortly after a
       suspicious auth event (the user's "fail -> new admin -> ..." chain) =====

       Two build constraints shape this:
       1) if_matched_group is fine in PRODUCTION (it only fails to accumulate inside
          wazuh-logtest). Either operator works on the live manager; this draft uses
          if_matched_sid - which takes exactly ONE rule id - for a precise,
          logtest-testable chain.
       2) Wazuh frequency composites are PAIRWISE and have no host-scoping operator
          that is confirmed on this build, so a single rule cannot safely express
          "...on the SAME host" across a usermod event and an SSH-failure event.

       RECOMMENDED IMPLEMENTATION (robust, no exotic operators):
       keep each leg as its own TIER-1 atomic rule so the SEQUENCE is visible on
       the dashboard within one incident timeline, even though no single composite
       fires the whole chain:
         - 111020/111000  (successful auth after a failure burst)   already Tier-1
         - 111030         (user added to wheel/sudo/adm)            promote to email
         - 111100/111240  (sudoers / PAM change)                    already Tier-1
       That gives you the same investigative signal the composite would, without a
       fragile pairwise correlation.

       If you still want a single fired alert, validate this draft with
       wazuh-logtest first (confirm if_matched_sid accumulation across the two
       source rules on one agent), then move it into a <group> and analysisd -t:

       DRAFT FIXED 2026-07-08: frequency="1" is REJECTED by this build (valid
       range 2..9999 — the documented cause of the cryptic API "1113 XML syntax
       error"). frequency="2" is the legal minimum, but it means TWO prior 111000
       events are required before 111030 fires this — a single takeover login
       followed by a group add will NOT trip it. That constraint is exactly why
       the atomic-rules implementation above is the recommended one.

       <rule id="111500" level="13" frequency="2" timeframe="600">
         <if_sid>111030</if_sid>
         <if_matched_sid>111000</if_matched_sid>
         <description>PrivEsc chain: user added to a privileged group on $(agent.name) shortly after a brute-force-then-success login — probable hands-on-keyboard escalation</description>
         <mitre><id>T1078</id><id>T1098</id></mitre>
         <group>privilege_escalation,attack_success,correlation,</group>
       </rule>
  -->

</group>
